<abstract>
<p>We document a <bold>mid-elevation peak</bold> in species richness for vascular plants, birds, and insects across eight tropical mountains spanning three continents.</p>
</abstract>
</article-meta>
</front>
<body>
<sec>
<title>Introduction</title>
<p>The relationship between elevation and species richness has been debated since <italic>Humboldt</italic> first documented vegetational zones on equatorial peaks (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="r1">Humboldt, 1807</xref>). Two competing patterns — monotonic decline vs. mid-elevation peak — dominate the literature.</p>
</sec>
<sec>
<title>Methods</title>
<p>Standardized surveys were conducted at 200 m elevational intervals from 200 to 4000 m asl on each mountain.</p>
</sec>
<sec>
<title>Results</title>
<p>The mid-elevation peak was statistically supported in 22 of 24 taxon × mountain combinations:</p>
<table-wrap>
<label>Table 1</label>
<caption><p>Peak elevation and richness by taxon group</p></caption>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Taxon</th><th>Peak Elevation (m)</th><th>Peak Richness</th><th>Pattern</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Vascular plants</td><td>1200–1800</td><td>142 ± 28</td><td>Mid-peak</td></tr>
<tr><td>Birds</td><td>1000–1600</td><td>98 ± 19</td><td>Mid-peak</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insects</td><td>800–1400</td><td>234 ± 67</td><td>Mid-peak</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</table-wrap>
</sec>
